# {{PROJECT_NAME}} — the project context repository

*For everyone on the project. Part 1 needs no technical background; Part 2 is for
people who work in the code repositories.* Repository owner: {{OWNER}}. {{DATE}}.

## Part 1 — For everyone

**Why it exists.** Project knowledge — scope, decisions and their reasons, what a word
means to the client, who answers for what — usually lives in chats, slides and heads.
Every newcomer, every hand-over and every AI assistant rediscovers it.
`{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-context` is the one place where **{{PROJECT_NAME}}** keeps *what is true
today*, kept current by named people. It is also what the team's AI assistants read
before they act, so a developer, a tester and an analyst work from the same facts.

**What is in it.** Two kinds of area, plus two supporting files.

| Area | Answers |
| :-- | :-- |
| Baseline | What is the project held to? Scope, assumptions, risks, non-functional requirements. |
| Architecture | How is the system shaped, now and in the target state? |
| Product | What are we building and why? |
| Delivery | How is the project run? |
| Quality | How do we know it works? |
| Backend, Frontend, Design | How each delivery layer works, and what it has settled. |
| Decisions | Every decision, one folder, with a scope column. Only accepted ones bind. |
| Glossary | What does each term mean here? |

**Three principles.**

1. *State of today.* A document describes the current agreed state; history is kept by
   version control and decision records, not old copies. `DEPRECATED` at the top means
   history.
2. *Decisions are recorded once, with reasons.* What was decided, the alternatives, why
   they lost. They all live in one folder, so there is one place to look and one number
   sequence. Only `Accepted` records bind. This stops the same debate every quarter.
3. *One area, one owner.* The owner keeps the area true and reviews every change to
   it. One person may own several areas; no area has two owners or none. This is what
   stops someone changing an area they do not understand, and what makes owners care.

**How to use it without writing code.** Open `docs/README.md`, pick the area, follow
its README. To check a decision, open `docs/decisions/` and read the index: `Accepted`
is in force, anything else is history, and the `Scope` column says which layer it
concerns. To change something, contact the owner named in the area table. A PDF you
receive is a snapshot; the repository is the source.

**What it is not.** Not the backlog tool, not the chat, not the design tool, not the
code. It ties those together and points at them.

## Part 2 — For engineers

**Layout on your machine.** Every repository sits in one project catalog, with the
context repository beside them:

```text
{{PROJECT_SLUG}}/
├── CLAUDE.md                  # loads in every session below; points at the context repo
├── {{PROJECT_SLUG}}-context/  # knowledge base + marketplace
├── <repo-a>/
└── <repo-b>/
```

The layout is a contract: the shared plugin is served from `../{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-context`,
and Claude Code loads the catalog's `CLAUDE.md` in any session started below it.

**One repository, two roles.** Plain markdown under `docs/` and `conventions/` is the
knowledge base — edited by people, reviewed by owners, never compiled. The
`.claude-plugin/marketplace.json` publishes `plugins/{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-shared/`, the one
plugin carrying the team's shared skills, agents and hooks.

**The four skills.** Every name carries the project, so with several projects installed
the `/` menu says which one you are reaching for.

| Skill | Does |
| :-- | :-- |
| `/{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-shared:{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-context` | Reads the knowledge base before work that depends on project facts |
| `/{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-shared:{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-knowledge` | Adds or updates a document in the owning workspace, index and reviewer included |
| `/{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-shared:{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-links` | Repairs broken links, bad filenames and misrendering tables |
| `/{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-shared:{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-space` | Creates a workspace: folder, README, `CLAUDE.md` with an owner, area-map row |

Decision records are deliberately not among them: `/tsh-core:managing-decision-records`
owns that format, and `conventions/decisions.md` matches it, so there is one standard
instead of two drifting apart. **What matters is where the record lands.** Every record
for this project goes to `{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-context/docs/decisions/`, never into a code
repository — from a code repository that is `../{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-context/docs/decisions/`.
Give that path to the skill as the archive location. The catalog's `CLAUDE.md` states
this rule and loads in every session started below it, so an assistant working in a code
repository already knows.

**Enable the plugin in a code repository.** Commit to its `.claude/settings.json`:

```json
{
  "extraKnownMarketplaces": {
    "{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-context": {
      "source": { "source": "directory", "path": "../{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-context" }
    }
  },
  "enabledPlugins": { "{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-shared@{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-context": true }
}
```

Start Claude Code there, accept the trust dialog once, check `/plugin list`. Two facts
worth knowing: a `settings.json` in the catalog folder does **not** reach the
repositories below it, which is why each repository carries its own; a `CLAUDE.md` in
the catalog **does** load below it. Without the sibling checkout the plugin cannot
load, and the skills say so instead of guessing.

**The quality gate.** Two scripts, run before a pull request:

```shell
python3 scripts/check_links.py
python3 scripts/check_tables.py
```

The first catches links that no longer resolve and filenames that are not kebab-case.
The second catches tables that render wrongly — a row one cell short shifts every later
cell into the wrong column, which nothing warns you about and readers see first.
`{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-links` runs both and repairs what it finds.

**Where a new skill goes.** Needed by more than one role or repository → this plugin,
named `{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-<what-it-does>`. One repository only → that repository's
`.claude/`. Ties go to the repository.

**A day with it.** You start Claude Code in a code repository. The catalog `CLAUDE.md`
and the shared plugin are already there. The assistant reads the area map and the
decision index and works from accepted facts. A document and the code disagree: it
reports both, you raise it with the owner. Something is learned worth keeping: you run
the knowledge skill and it lands in the right workspace with the right reviewer. A
decision is made: you propose a record, the owner confirms its status.

**Open point.** How and by whom this folder becomes a git repository is a pending
decision. Until then the folder is plain files, the checker scripts are run by hand,
and the side-by-side layout is the contract.
